---
/**
 * The Home page — phase 4 item 3, and mostly composition.
 *
 * ── FOUR BANDS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 *   Hero            this file — a statement and one button, on the accent ground
 *   Insights        Articles and Notes, side by side           (not yet built)
 *   Topics          Selected Topics and Genres, taxonomy-driven (not yet built)
 *   Work With Me    <WorkWithMeBand>, already built             (not yet wired)
 *
 * ── `/` IS LOAD-BEARING BEYOND BEING THE HOME PAGE ──────────────────────────
 *
 * The masthead's `rel="home"` and, more importantly, the `<link rel="author" href="/">`
 * on all 46 pages both resolve here. That author link is inert until this page carries
 * the site's representative `h-card`, which is why CLAUDE.md files the h-card under the
 * home page rather than under phase 8's microformats work. It lands with the last band.
 *
 * The throwaway specimen that used to occupy this route moved to `/specimen/`, not
 * deleted — it is still the quickest way to eyeball the type and card system.
 */
import {loadQuery} from '../sanity/load-query'
import {SINGLETON_WORK_BAND_QUERY} from '../sanity/queries/singletons'
import {
  GENRE_TREE_QUERY,
  HOME_AUTHOR_QUERY,
  HOME_INSIGHTS_QUERY,
  HOME_TAXONOMY_QUERY,
} from '../sanity/queries/home'
import {imageAttrs} from '../sanity/image'
import {partitionGenres, pluralizeGenre} from '../lib/genres'
import {selectTopics} from '../lib/topics'
import {slugify} from '../lib/slug'
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Band from '../components/Band.astro'
import Grid from '../components/Grid.astro'
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import Prose from '../components/prose/Prose.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import ArticleCard from '../components/cards/ArticleCard.astro'
import NoteCard from '../components/cards/NoteCard.astro'
import WorkWithMeBand from '../components/bands/WorkWithMeBand.astro'

/*
  ── HOW MANY THE INSIGHTS BAND SHOWS. TUNE HERE. ────────────────────────────

  Andy's call (2026-09-03): easy to adjust, but not a CMS field — nobody needs to change
  it from the Studio, and a setting nobody uses is a setting that can be set wrong. The
  board draws four and five.

  They reach GROQ as `[0...$n]` slice bounds, so changing a number here changes what is
  FETCHED rather than what is rendered from an over-large result.
*/
const ARTICLE_COUNT = 4
const NOTE_COUNT = 5

/*
  ── TWO ROUND TRIPS, AND THE SECOND DEPENDS ON THE FIRST ────────────────────

  The genre tree has to come back before the partition exists, and the partition is what
  the Insights query filters on — so these cannot be a `Promise.all`. The singleton can,
  and is, because it depends on neither.
*/
const [page, genreTree, author] = await Promise.all([
  loadQuery(SINGLETON_WORK_BAND_QUERY, {slug: 'home'}),
  loadQuery(GENRE_TREE_QUERY),
  loadQuery(HOME_AUTHOR_QUERY),
])

/*
  ── THE REPRESENTATIVE h-card's PHOTO ───────────────────────────────────────

  This page is where `<link rel="author" href="/">` — emitted on all 46 pages — finally
  resolves. <Masthead> carries the card when asked; see its own note for why it is opt-in
  and why the name, role and URL are marked up on visible text rather than invented.

  ── SQUARE, BECAUSE AN AVATAR IS SQUARE ─────────────────────────────────────

  `authorImage` is the landscape studio portrait, 1262x823. A consumer showing a
  webmention renders this small and almost always in a square or a circle, so it is
  cropped here rather than left for someone else's CSS to cut arbitrarily — `aspect: '1/1'`
  makes the hotspot live, which is the whole reason the field has one.

  `imageAttrs` builds a srcset this has no use for; `.src` is the single URL `u-photo`
  wants. Null when the field is unset, and <Masthead> then omits the property rather than
  emitting an empty one — an h-card with a name and a URL is still valid and still
  resolves, it just carries no avatar.
*/
const authorPhoto = author?.authorImage?.asset
  ? (imageAttrs(author.authorImage, {aspect: '1/1'})?.src ?? null)
  : null

const {articleGenres, noteGenres, documentGenres} = partitionGenres(genreTree)

const [insights, taxonomy] = await Promise.all([
  loadQuery(HOME_INSIGHTS_QUERY, {
    articleGenres,
    noteGenres,
    articleCount: ARTICLE_COUNT,
    noteCount: NOTE_COUNT,
  }),
  loadQuery(HOME_TAXONOMY_QUERY, {documentGenres}),
])

/*
  ── THE TOPICS BAND'S TWO LISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  Genres are every Document-branch concept something is actually tagged with, shown as
  plurals because each labels a group. The COUNT is what filters — a genre added to the
  taxonomy appears here the moment content carries it, and disappears again if that
  content goes away, with no code change. It also removes the Document top concept, which
  is in `documentGenres` but is never tagged.

  ── THE LINK CARRIES THE SINGULAR, THE LABEL SHOWS THE PLURAL ──────────────

  `/insights/?genre=book-note`, not `book-notes`. The facet index the Insights page filters
  against is keyed on the slugified prefLabel, so pluralising the href would produce a
  filter that matches nothing — a link that looks right and quietly returns an empty index.
  Slugging `prefLabel` rather than the display label is what keeps them in step.
*/
const genres = taxonomy.genres
  .flatMap((genre) => (genre.prefLabel && genre.count > 0 ? [genre.prefLabel] : []))
  .map((prefLabel) => ({label: pluralizeGenre(prefLabel), slug: slugify(prefLabel)}))
  .sort((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label))

/*
  ── EXACTLY TWICE AS MANY TOPICS AS GENRES ──────────────────────────────────

  Andy's rule (2026-09-03), and it is a ratio rather than a number so the two columns stay
  in proportion as the vocabulary grows: 7 genres today, so 14 topics. The board draws 15
  against 8, which is the same shape from before the Presentation-branch genres were
  unpublished.

  Deriving it means the band cannot drift into a lopsided pair, and there is no second
  constant to update when a genre is added.
*/
const topics = selectTopics(taxonomy.topics, genres.length * 2).map((topic) => ({
  label: topic.label,
  slug: slugify(topic.label),
}))

/*
  ── THE CARD MAPPING IS INLINE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED ─────────────────

  The Insights index builds the same three things — an `/insights/` href, a title fallback,
  and a Clipping's source domain — and this is a second consumer, which is usually this
  project's trigger to extract. It is not extracted, because the two callers need
  different SHAPES: the index also carries facet slugs and masonry hints that exist only
  there, so a shared helper would either return fields Home ignores or take a parameter
  saying which caller it is.

  What would justify extracting is a rule that is invisible when wrong — the reason
  `formatDate` is a module. A wrong href is visible the first time anyone clicks it.

  `slug` is narrowed rather than asserted: the query filters on `defined(slug.current)`
  but TypeGen cannot see the filter, so it types the projection `string | null`.
*/
const articleCards = insights.articles.flatMap((item) =>
  item.slug
    ? [
        {
          href: `/insights/${item.slug}/`,
          title: item.title ?? 'Untitled',
          genre: item.genre,
          date: item.pubDate,
          description: item.shortDescription,
          image: item.heroImage,
        },
      ]
    : [],
)

const noteCards = insights.notes.flatMap((item) =>
  item.slug
    ? [
        {
          href: `/insights/${item.slug}/`,
          title: item.title ?? 'Untitled',
          genre: item.genre,
          date: item.pubDate,
          description: item.shortDescription,
          /* A Clipping shows its source where a plain Note shows its description —
             the ref's PRESENCE selects that, not the genre label, matching NoteCard. */
          sourceDomain: item.clipRef?.publisher ?? null,
        },
      ]
    : [],
)
---

<BaseLayout title="Andy Fitzgerald — Information Architect" description={page?.lede ?? undefined}>
  <Masthead microformats photo={authorPhoto} />

  <main>
    {
      /*
        ── THE HERO TAKES THE ACCENT GROUND ONLY FROM lg ───────────────────────

        The two boards genuinely differ, and it is a design rule rather than drift
        (Andy, 2026-09-03): below lg the stacked navigation is ITSELF accent-coloured, so
        an accent hero would run into it with no edge between them. The hero therefore
        sits on the page ground until the nav unstacks at 1024, and takes the accent band
        above it.

        ── WHICH IS WHY `tone` IS NOT USED, AND THE PAGE OWNS THE FILL ─────────

        `tone` is a prop, so it becomes a static attribute and cannot vary by viewport.
        The obvious fix — teaching <Band> a `toneFrom` — was considered and rejected:
        Home is the only page with a hero band, and this is the only band in the design
        that changes tone at a breakpoint, so a general prop would be built for a second
        consumer that is not coming.

        The usual objection to a page painting its own band is Band's own: "changing a
        band's fill is never a one-property change — the text on it has to move in the
        same breath," which is why `tone="accent"` re-points ELEVEN declarations. That
        objection mostly does not apply here, and the reason is worth stating: those
        eleven exist because a band holds arbitrary slotted content — links, borders,
        icons, muted text, quotes. This band holds an h1 and a button. Two of the eleven
        are reachable, so two is what the style block sets.

        `space="band"` with no override: the board draws 64 top and bottom at 1440 and
        32 at 375, which is exactly what `--rhythm-band` clamps between.
      */
    }
    <Band as="section" class="hero-band">
      <Grid>
        <div class="hero">
          {
            /*
              ── THE STATEMENT IS THE h1 ─────────────────────────────────────

              The board draws no other heading on this page, and a page with no h1 has
              no accessible name for its main region and a broken document outline. The
              statement is what the page is about, so it is the honest h1 rather than an
              invented "Home" nobody wants to read.

              It takes DESIGN.md's `display` role, NOT base.css's h1 — see the style
              block for what that costs. Figma binds this node to the `Display` text
              style, which is the confirmation that it is a role and not a one-off.

              <Prose> renders it because `heroCopy` is Portable Text and may carry a
              mark. It emits a <p>, so the h1 wraps it rather than replacing it — the
              alternative is a serializer override for one block on one page.
            */
          }
          <h1 class="hero-statement"><Prose value={page?.heroCopy} /></h1>

          {
            /* `primary` is the BASE state, which mobile-first makes the right default:
               it is what the board draws below lg, on the page ground. The style block
               gives it the ghost treatment from lg, where it lands on the accent band.

               The destination is fixed rather than content — there is one About page,
               the same reasoning WorkWithMeBand gives for hardcoding /consulting. */
          }
          <Button href="/about" variant="primary" class="hero-cta">Read more about me</Button>
        </div>
      </Grid>
    </Band>

    {
      /*
        ── THE INSIGHTS BAND ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

        Two columns on the board's asymmetric split — 571 + skip-1 + 316, closing 996
        exactly — which is the same split the Insights index uses for its facet groups
        and the Work With Me band uses for its copy and logos.

        <div>s RATHER THAN NAMED <section>s. Each column is a thematic grouping and a
        <section> would be defensible, but a NAMED one is a `region` landmark, and
        CaseStudyEntry already settled that case: landmarks for content nothing navigates
        to are noise. The Reviews page went the other way because its rail lists exactly
        those four sections; nothing points at these two. The h2s carry the outline.
      */
    }
    <Band as="section" class="insights-band">
      <Grid>
        <div class="insights-col">
          {
            /*
              ── THE BLURBS STAY IN THE TEMPLATE. SETTLED, NOT DEFERRED ────────

              Both column blurbs are hardcoded, following WorkWithMeBand's precedent for
              copy that never varies. Raised as a possible singleton field, since unlike
              a heading these are sentences of editorial prose — and decided against
              (Andy, 2026-09-04): they describe the SECTIONS this template draws, not the
              content underneath them, so they change when the template changes and not
              when the writing does. A field would be a place to set them wrong.
            */
          }
          <h2>Articles</h2>
          <p class="col-lede">
            Perspectives, guides, and case studies about information architecture for modern content
            ecosystems.
          </p>

          <div class="card-list">
            {
              articleCards.map((card) => (
                <ArticleCard
                  as="h3"
                  href={card.href}
                  title={card.title}
                  genre={card.genre}
                  date={card.date}
                  description={card.description}
                  image={card.image}
                />
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="insights-col">
          <h2>Notes</h2>
          <p class="col-lede">Brief observations and clippings from around the web.</p>

          <div class="card-list note-list">
            {
              noteCards.map((card) => (
                <NoteCard
                  as="h3"
                  href={card.href}
                  title={card.title}
                  genre={card.genre}
                  date={card.date}
                  description={card.description}
                  sourceDomain={card.sourceDomain}
                />
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </Grid>
    </Band>

    {
      /*
        ── THE TOPICS BAND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        `space="section"` for the board's 96 above and 64 at 375 — the same value every
        APPENDED band takes, which is what this is: the page's content is done and these
        are ways back into it.

        `tone="accent"` UNCONDITIONALLY, unlike the hero. Both Figma variants carry
        `color/accent/band`, so there is nothing viewport-dependent to express here and
        the prop does the whole job — including re-pointing `--color-link-hover`, which is
        what makes these links legible on the fill without this file naming a colour.
        Footer.astro is the precedent: a real accent band holding a <nav>.

        <nav> PER LIST, each named by its own visible heading. Two navigations, not one,
        because no single label covers "Selected Topics" and "Genres" — the same shape and
        the same reasoning the Insights index arrived at for its two facet groups. These
        ARE navigation, so unlike the Insights band's columns the landmark is earned.
      */
    }
    <Band as="section" space="section" tone="accent" class="topics-band">
      <Grid>
        <nav class="topics-col" aria-labelledby="home-topics">
          <h2 id="home-topics">Selected Topics</h2>
          <ul class="topic-grid" role="list">
            {
              topics.map((topic) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`/insights/?topic=${topic.slug}`}>{topic.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <nav class="genres-col" aria-labelledby="home-genres">
          <h2 id="home-genres">Genres</h2>
          <ul class="genre-list" role="list">
            {
              genres.map((genre) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`/insights/?genre=${genre.slug}`}>{genre.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </Grid>
    </Band>

    {
      /*
        ── WORK WITH ME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        The last band, and the only one on this page that was already built — it takes its
        own <Band>, <Grid>, tone and spacing, so there is nothing to compose around it.

        `workBand` arrives already RESOLVED. SINGLETON_WORK_BAND_QUERY runs the three-tier
        ladder in GROQ — the document's own `bands`, then a page-type override in Settings,
        then the Settings default — so this page never learns which tier produced it. Home
        sets no `bands` of its own and Settings has no override for singletons, so it lands
        on the site default, which is exactly the arrangement fragments.ts documents.

        Rendered only when the ladder produced something. There is a site default, so the
        only way to reach null is Settings carrying no Work With Me band at all — in which
        case a heading and a button over empty space would be worse than no band.
      */
    }
    {
      page?.workBand && (
        <WorkWithMeBand message={page.workBand.message} clientLogos={page.workBand.clientLogos} />
      )
    }
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /*
   * ── FULL CONTENT WIDTH, AND THE MEASURE COMES FROM THE ROLE ────────────────
   *
   * `--col-full`, because there is no rail here and the board runs the statement the
   * whole 996. DESIGN.md puts `display` at "~43 characters at 35px", and at 996 the
   * real string lands at 42 per line over three lines — so the column IS the measure
   * and a `ch` cap would only be a second, disagreeing statement of it.
   *
   * ── A COLUMN THAT END-ALIGNS, WHICH IS WHAT PUTS THE BUTTON RIGHT ──────────
   *
   * Mobile-first per tokens.css. The mobile board centres both the statement and the
   * button; the desktop board ranges the statement left and pushes the button to the
   * right edge — one frame with `counterAxisAlignItems: MAX`, not two alignments.
   *
   * `align-items` moves the button and `text-align` sets the statement, and the two are
   * not redundant: the statement is a full-width block whose TEXT ranges left at
   * desktop, while the button is a hug-width item that moves to the end. `align-items`
   * alone would leave the statement ranged left at both widths.
   *
   * 32 is `--space-4`, the board's gap at both widths.
   */
  .hero {
    grid-column: var(--col-full);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    text-align: center;
  }

  /*
   * ── EVERYTHING ABOUT THE HERO CHANGES AT lg, IN ONE MOVE ───────────────────
   *
   * lg and not md, and the reason is the NAVIGATION: below 1024 it is stacked and
   * accent-coloured, so an accent hero would run into it with no edge between them
   * (Andy, 2026-09-03). The hero holds its narrow presentation until the nav unstacks.
   *
   * The alignment sat at md in a first pass, which left 768–1023 in a state no board
   * draws — statement ranged left with a right-hand button, on the page ground. Moved
   * here so the whole band turns over at one width rather than in two steps.
   *
   * ONE BLOCK, NOT TWO, because that is the claim: there is a single transition. Two
   * `@container` blocks at the same width would read as a coincidence and invite the
   * next person to move one of them.
   *
   * ── THE FILL IS TWO DECLARATIONS, NOT <Band>'s ELEVEN ──────────────────────
   *
   * The difference is what this band holds. `--color-text-heading` is the only role
   * token anything inside reads: the h1 takes it from base.css's `:is(h1, h2, h3, h4)`,
   * and the button sets its own colour. There is no link, border, icon, muted text or
   * quote in here to re-point for.
   *
   * `--color-text` is deliberately NOT set. Nothing in this band renders body copy, and
   * re-pointing a token no element reads is how a bundle grows into something that has
   * to be kept in step with a component it no longer matches.
   *
   * No competing rule to outrank: <Band> styles `surface`, `logo` and `accent` and
   * leaves its default `page` tone unstyled, so this adds rather than overrides.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .hero {
      align-items: flex-end;
      text-align: start;
    }

    .hero-band {
      background-color: var(--color-surface-accent);
      --color-text-heading: var(--color-text-on-accent);
    }

    /*
     * ── AND THE BUTTON BECOMES THE GHOST ────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * `primary`'s fill IS `--color-surface-accent`, so on this band it would be an
     * invisible box with white text. That is precisely what <Button>'s `ghost` variant
     * exists for, and these four blocks are its rules, restated here because `variant`
     * is a static attribute and one hero does not justify a `variantFrom` prop.
     *
     * THE COMPOUND SELECTOR IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT STYLE. `.button[data-variant='primary']`
     * carries <Button>'s own scope attribute, so it is three simple selectors at
     * (0,3,0); a page reaches the component's root with one class plus one scope
     * attribute at (0,2,0) and loses silently, wherever it sits in the source. Matching
     * the class AND the variant attribute here takes it to (0,4,0), which wins on
     * specificity rather than on the bundler's ordering. Same trap `Band`'s `spaceEnd`
     * documents.
     *
     * The focus pair is REVERSED against base.css's `2px offset, 4px ring`, and that is
     * the ghost's own fix rather than a copying slip: on this ground the default paints
     * a blue-500 ring onto a blue-500 band, invisible on exactly the control that most
     * needs it. Reversed, the inner ring reads against the button and the outer white
     * one against the band.
     */
    .button.hero-cta[data-variant='primary'] {
      background-color: var(--color-surface-accent);
      border-color: var(--color-border-on-accent);
      color: var(--color-text-on-accent);
    }

    .button.hero-cta[data-variant='primary']:hover {
      background-color: var(--color-surface);
      color: var(--color-surface-accent);
    }

    .button.hero-cta[data-variant='primary']:active {
      background-color: var(--color-surface-pressed);
      color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    }

    .button.hero-cta[data-variant='primary']:focus-visible {
      box-shadow:
        0 0 0 2px var(--color-focus-ring),
        0 0 0 4px var(--color-focus-offset);
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── DESIGN.md's `display` ROLE, WHICH FIGHTS THE HEADING DEFAULTS ──────────
   *
   * Step 5 (30 -> 35.15625), weight 500, leading 1.4, tracking 0 — measured off the
   * board's `Display` text style, and matching DESIGN.md's leading table, where
   * `display` is the "statement" register at 1.4.
   *
   * THREE OF THESE FOUR EXIST TO UNDO base.css, which is worth saying because a first
   * draft of this file left them out on the theory that a serif statement would inherit
   * body's family. It does not: `:is(h1, h2, h3, h4)` sets `font-family`,
   * `font-weight: 700` and `color`, so an h1 arrives here as Lato Bold no matter what
   * the element is being used to say.
   *
   *   font-family    back to `--font-prose`. Never a literal — CLAUDE.md's standing
   *                  rule, and Astro's Fonts API scopes the real family name anyway.
   *   font-weight    500, the only `fontWeight: 500` role in DESIGN.md, carried by
   *                  Noto Serif's variable axis rather than by a fifth static face.
   *   letter-spacing h1 sets -0.015em for Lato at display sizes. Noto Serif takes 0% at
   *                  every size, so this is a cancellation, not a value.
   *
   * NO `color`, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE TONE SWITCH FREE. The h1 takes
   * `--color-text-heading` from base.css, so it is dark on the page ground below lg and
   * white above it for the single reason that the band re-points that one token. Stating
   * a colour here would pin it to one of the two grounds and break the other.
   *
   * Specificity is not close: `:is(h1, h2, h3, h4)` takes its highest argument, which is
   * an element at (0,0,1), against this class plus its scope attribute at (0,2,0).
   */
  .hero-statement {
    font-family: var(--font-prose);
    font-size: var(--font-size-5);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: normal;
  }

  /* ── The Insights band ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * Mobile-first: one column, both full width, Articles then Notes — the mobile board's
   * order, which is also the DOM order, so nothing is reordered.
   *
   * ── THE SPLIT IS AT lg, AND md WAS MEASURABLY TOO EARLY ───────────────────
   *
   * A first pass split at md, matching the Insights index's facet groups on the same 7/4
   * columns. It overflowed the page horizontally at 768 — 770px of content in a 768px
   * viewport — and the outdent was not the cause: removing it changed nothing.
   *
   * A NOTE CARD HAS A MINIMUM WIDTH AND THE 4-COLUMN TRACK IS BELOW IT. The eyebrow and
   * the source line do not wrap, so the card's min-content width measures about 278px
   * including its padding. At 768 the notes track is 229px, so the card is forced wider
   * than its own column and spills through the 16px grid margin. At 1024 the track is
   * 315px and it fits, which is why that width measured clean.
   *
   * The index gets away with md because its columns hold facet CHIPS, which wrap freely.
   * Cards do not. So this is a different question wearing the same 7/4 split.
   *
   * Landing on lg also puts the whole page on one breakpoint: the hero changes there
   * because the navigation does, and below it Home is uniformly the narrow presentation
   * the mobile board draws.
   */
  .insights-col {
    grid-column: var(--col-full);
  }

  /*
   * 16 from the heading to its lede, 48 from the lede to the cards. Both from the board,
   * and both are ramp values rather than new numbers: `heading-close` is what binds a
   * heading to what follows it, and 48 is the step the Reviews page uses between a
   * group heading and its content.
   *
   * Written as margins rather than a `gap` on the column because the three parts want two
   * different values, which is the same reason the client block on Reviews uses margins.
   */
  .col-lede {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
  }

  .card-list {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .insights-col:first-of-type {
      grid-column: 1 / span 7;
    }

    .insights-col:last-of-type {
      grid-column: 9 / span 4;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── THE NOTE CARDS OUTDENT, AND THE CARD SAYS THIS IS OURS TO DECIDE ───────
   *
   * NoteCard's header: "Its outdent, which is the CONTEXT's call. On the home page it is
   * pulled out by `card-pad` so its text sits flush with the column edge and the hover box
   * bleeds past it." DESIGN.md states it as "outdent the card, never the column."
   *
   * `--card-pad` is 16, so a 316 column carries 348 cards — exactly what the board's own
   * frame name says: "Note list — 348 outdent (span-4 + 2x card/pad)". The negative margin
   * reads the same token the card pads with, so the two cannot drift.
   *
   * ── IT WAITS FOR THE PAGE MARGIN TO HAVE ROOM, NOT FOR THE SPLIT ───────────
   *
   * The outdent bleeds 16px OUTWARD, and on the end side it bleeds into the page margin.
   * `--grid-margin` is a formula that floors at 16, so from md up to 1028 the margin IS
   * 16 and the bleed consumes every pixel of it — the card lands flush against the
   * viewport edge. Nothing overflows; it just looks wrong on a tablet (Andy, on screen).
   *
   *   vw     1024   1028   1029   1040   1060   1100   1440
   *   margin   16     16   16.5     22     32     52    222
   *   left      0      0    0.5      6     16     36    206
   *
   * So the condition is not the column split — it is whether the margin can afford the
   * bleed, and that is a different width. 1028 is where `(100vw - 996) / 2` finally
   * exceeds the 16px floor; clearance then ramps in, reaching a full `card-pad` at 1060.
   *
   * ── A MEDIA QUERY, WHERE tokens.css PREFERS A SIZE CONTAINER QUERY ─────────
   *
   * tokens.css calls this a MINOR breakpoint — "a single component adjusting to the space
   * IT has" — and says to prefer a real size container query. That does not work here,
   * and the reason is worth recording rather than looking like the rule was ignored.
   *
   * A size query answers to the container's own inline size, and `.grid`'s is CAPPED at
   * 996: it reads identically at 1028 and at 1440. The quantity that varies is the margin
   * OUTSIDE the grid, which no descendant container can see. Querying an ancestor that
   * does span the viewport would mean putting `container-type: inline-size` on <main> or
   * the band, which also changes what every `@container style(…)` inside them resolves
   * against — a wide blast radius for one negative margin.
   *
   * The condition is genuinely viewport-derived, so a media query states it honestly.
   * 64.25rem is 1028px at the 16px root this project assumes elsewhere for the grid
   * formula, and it is NOT a new major breakpoint: nothing else keys off it.
   */
  @media (min-width: 64.25rem) {
    .note-list {
      margin-inline: calc(var(--card-pad) * -1);
    }
  }

  /* ── The Topics band ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * Mobile-first: both lists full width, stacked, Topics then Genres — the mobile board's
   * order and the DOM's. The 7/4 split arrives at md, where these lists differ from the
   * Insights band above them: a topic is a short link that wraps freely, so a narrow
   * column costs it nothing. That is the same reason the Insights INDEX can split its
   * facet groups at md while its cards cannot.
   */
  /*
   * `container-type: inline-size` makes each column the reference for its own list's
   * column count — see the note on the query below. It is safe on a grid item: the
   * inline size still comes from the track, and block size is not contained, so the list
   * grows the column normally.
   */
  .topics-col,
  .genres-col {
    container-type: inline-size;
    grid-column: var(--col-full);
  }

  /* base.css keeps the UA's list indent for prose; a nav is not prose. */
  .topic-grid,
  .genre-list {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /*
   * ── ROW-MAJOR, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE ALPHABET READ CORRECTLY ────────────
   *
   * Both lists are TWO COLUMNS filled LEFT TO RIGHT THEN DOWN, so the a-z runs across
   * each row rather than down the first column. Andy's call (2026-09-03), citing the
   * footer's site index — which does behave that way, though its own comment claims the
   * opposite. `grid-auto-flow: column` appears nowhere in Footer.astro; the default `row`
   * is doing the work, and the comment is stale rather than the code being wrong.
   *
   * NOTHING HERE SETS `grid-auto-flow`, and that is the point: row-major IS the default,
   * so the DOM order is the reading order and the alphabet is already correct in the
   * markup. A screen reader and a sighted reader get the same sequence, which is the
   * property the Insights masonry was rebuilt to recover.
   *
   * `--grid-gutter-content` is the column gap, and it already carries the board's two
   * values: DESIGN.md reserves it for gaps between real content columns and defines it
   * "larger on mobile, not smaller" — 32 stacked, 24 at md. The boards draw exactly that,
   * 156 + 32 + 156 at 375 and 274 + 24 + 274 at 1440. Nothing here restates either
   * number.
   */
  /* One column is the base case, per tokens.css's mobile-first rule: an engine that never
     evaluates the query below must be left with the narrow layout. */
  .topic-grid,
  .genre-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter-content);
  }

  /*
   * ── A SIZE CONTAINER QUERY, WHICH IS THE MINOR-BREAKPOINT MODEL ───────────
   *
   * tokens.css: a MINOR breakpoint is "a single component adjusting to the space IT has",
   * and it says to prefer a real size container query. This is the first one in the build,
   * and it is the right tool because the question is genuinely local — does THIS list's
   * column have room for two of its longest label?
   *
   * ── 26rem IS MEASURED, AND IT IS ALSO VERY NEARLY THE ONLY VALUE ──────────
   *
   * The longest labels are "Information Architecture" (198px at the desktop step) and
   * "Conference Themes" (158px). Two columns plus the gutter therefore need about 416 and
   * 348 of container. The link step is fluid, so the labels are ~5% narrower where this
   * threshold bites, which the measurement accounts for:
   *
   *     vw    container   gap   topic col   longest topic
   *     375     343        32     155.5         187      wraps  -> one column
   *     448     416        32     192           188      fits   -> two columns
   *     768     419        24     197.5         192      fits
   *    1440     571        24     273.5         198      fits
   *
   * The window is narrow and worth naming, because the obvious larger number breaks
   * something invisible: the 7-column TRACK at 768 is 419px, which is NARROWER than the
   * stacked column at 475px viewport (443). Any threshold above 419 would quietly collapse
   * the Topics list to one column at 768 as well. So 26rem is close to the ceiling, not a
   * round number picked for looks — and Andy's 475px viewport figure is not reachable this
   * way without that side effect. It switches at about 448px instead, which is where the
   * measurement says the wrapping actually stops.
   *
   * ── IT ALSO RETIRES THE GENRES SPECIAL CASE ───────────────────────────────
   *
   * The genre list used to carry an explicit md rule dropping it to one column, with a
   * comment explaining why it "inverts" — two columns stacked, one from md. That was a
   * hand-written statement of something the container now derives: its md track is 229px
   * and its lg track 316px, both under 26rem, so one column falls out on its own. The
   * inversion was never a rule, only a consequence of the column being narrow, and it
   * reads much better as one.
   */
  @container (inline-size >= 26rem) {
    .topic-grid,
    .genre-list {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .topics-col {
      grid-column: 1 / span 7;
    }

    .genres-col {
      grid-column: 9 / span 4;
    }
  }
</style>
